# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Project XML and JSON are SOURCE, not build output
#
# The Vivado/Vitis template above ignores *.xml and *.json wholesale. In this
# project course content *is* XML, and fixtures, MCP resources and package
# config are JSON, so that blanket rule made every new course package and test
# fixture need `git add -f` to commit. Re-include them by path: a stray Vivado
# report elsewhere in the tree stays ignored, while anything under the source
# trees is tracked normally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!llmgrader/**/*.xml
!llmgrader/**/*.json
!tests/**/*.xml
!tests/**/*.json
!docs/**/*.xml
!docs/**/*.json
!example_repo/**/*.xml
!example_repo/**/*.json
!soln_repos/*.xml

# ...with these exceptions, which are generated rather than authored. They
# have to come after the re-includes above: last matching rule wins.

# Live-model cost/latency report, rewritten on every `pytest tests/live` run.
tests/live/_report.json

# Rendered from the example XML by the course-build scripts.
example_repo/**/*.html
example_repo/**/*.pdf

# Built by `create_soln_pkg`. A directory exclude, so nothing inside it can be
# re-included by the rules above -- which is the intent.
example_repo_temp/
example_repo/soln_package/
example_repo/*.zip

# Jekyll builds the docs site; GitHub Pages does it for real, but a local
# `jekyll serve` would otherwise drop its JSON output into `git status`.
_site/
.jekyll-cache/
**/example_repo/autograder
**/example_repo/autograder/
docs/key.txt
local_data/

# Experiment directory
experiment/

# Test environment directory
*-test-env/

